#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5abcb7d-ec43-5980-8c4d-e215a1535a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359100} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8cf78d-d1eb-5cc7-8205-7dac1bca9ff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=7\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359101} {:answer/id #uuid "c6f5066f-e1b8-5a35-beba-55af2daad26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=8\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359102} {:answer/id #uuid "689bb84e-0b2d-5052-b589-6871ef8ea4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=6\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359103} {:answer/id #uuid "25ad8160-28d5-5781-add2-44055a3c04ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5abcb7d-ec43-5980-8c4d-e215a1535a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359100} :answer-field/id #uuid "bec9a6f7-c9fe-5a2c-a4a1-2312f01cb405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e63c32d-f7f6-5fb4-a61e-8ffdf954dfa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-138910" :question/problem "A body of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{2}-16t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-16t+5) \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-16 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =16 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b4b33c-fa3a-5e34-a592-6999b9feccc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=3\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359107} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcc3ead-5216-5a37-8f8f-f4c2ba997801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=4\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359108} {:answer/id #uuid "df798ab0-d00a-55db-84d6-4b2221a0f07a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=8\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359109} {:answer/id #uuid "3afa6e5d-8196-5565-b620-7fe7e3fe9f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359110} {:answer/id #uuid "f57f7845-d030-54c0-828e-ba3f108f18af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=10\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3afa6e5d-8196-5565-b620-7fe7e3fe9f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=5\\,\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359110} :answer-field/id #uuid "27cbe526-1d04-5086-ae08-08e982b90f4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "221d56ed-83a4-543c-a1f6-7af8b38ce036" :question/math-academy-id "q-138913" :question/problem "A body of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=(t-4){e}^{-t}$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-4){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}+(t-4)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(5-t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-t){e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ 5-t & =0 \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b443ba-c052-53be-a205-e4557529460e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\sec(t))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91b443ba-c052-53be-a205-e4557529460e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\sec(t))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359114} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc8570f7-ec30-5238-be01-255ea6bd9546" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7cd21afb-f40b-53c4-ad7d-67ffe3f6fab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345338" :question/problem "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e955e35e-c45f-53b9-9f95-332d3e9c648f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Force Acting on a Body Given Its Displacement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fefe3b53-f05d-512a-a0fe-9b2334b8e141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359117} {:answer/id #uuid "1aeaa308-cd0e-5644-b253-9b3a050dda88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1aeaa308-cd0e-5644-b253-9b3a050dda88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t}{(1+t^2)^2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359118} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5ae1eba-b1a1-511e-ba2d-0316cadded79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df1e6f0b-3cb8-5b84-956f-6e1aa296a8a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-345340" :question/problem "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-1}] \\\\  & =-{({t}^{2}+1)}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [-\\frac{2t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{t}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "266102e7-c8ef-55c9-8fcd-c3b6962f03b2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Moments When a Force Has Zero Magnitude"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e93ac296-e56c-5b3a-8af2-25f6b0d6d9ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e93ac296-e56c-5b3a-8af2-25f6b0d6d9ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359121} :answer-field/id #uuid "28cd5d84-cbbc-5c67-bdf6-4b9332087f7f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36d3c8af-adbd-575f-a078-a90af2f53575" :question/math-academy-id "q-345390" :question/problem "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons. At time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24$ meters.\n\nCalculate the time ${t}_{∗}$ at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude. Do not round the answer.\n\n${t}_{∗}=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{s}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(4{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}+12t+24) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-12t+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(12{t}^{2}-12t+12) \\\\  & =24t-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24t-12 & =0 \\\\ 24t & =12 \\\\ t & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at ${t}_{∗}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$"}]}
